  <p>
    Most anti-inflammatory drugs work by holding the immune system <em>down</em>. Resomelagon is
    built on a different idea: help it <em>finish</em>. Known through most of its development by
    the code <strong>AP1189</strong>, it is <a href="https://www.synactpharma.com">SynAct
    Pharma</a>’s investigational oral, once-daily melanocortin agonist — and it is the clearest
    bet in the whole <a href="/pipeline">melanocortin pipeline</a> on a modern idea called
    resolution pharmacology.<Cite n={1} /> The name is not an accident. The international
    nonproprietary name <em>resomelagon</em> carries a stem meant to flag exactly what it is
    supposed to do: act on the <strong>resolution</strong> of inflammation.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>Biased by design</h2>
  <p>
    Resomelagon aims at <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> and <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a>,
    the two melanocortin receptors most involved in calming <a href="/inflammation">inflammation</a>.
    What makes it unusual is that it is a <strong><a href="/effects">biased</a> agonist</strong>.
    A receptor is not a simple on/off switch; it can route its signal down more than one pathway,
    and a biased drug is one that deliberately favours some of those pathways over others.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    In resomelagon’s case the split is the entire point. In the defining laboratory work, AP1189
    switched on MC1R and MC3R but did <strong>not</strong> drive the classic cAMP second-messenger
    pathway — and instead drove <strong>ERK1/2</strong> phosphorylation, the arm tied to
    pro-resolving behaviour in immune cells.<Cite n={2} /> That one choice pays off twice. Because
    the pigment-building machinery runs through cAMP, the drug produced no melanogenesis in the
    lab — so it should not come with the skin-darkening that trails most{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-agonists">melanocortin agonists</a>. And because it does not act on{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>, the adrenal receptor, it does not raise cortisol the way
    the old melanocortin anti-inflammatory — <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> — does.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <Figure
