    <BiasedAgonism />
  </Figure>

  <h2>Resolution, not suppression</h2>
  <p>
    Ending an inflammatory response is not a matter of simply turning inflammation off. It is an
    active, built-in program: the immune system has to clear the dead and dying cells and the
    debris left behind, a step called <strong>resolution</strong>. Melanocortin signalling helps
    drive it — in particular through MC3R, which pushes macrophages to engulf spent neutrophils
    (efferocytosis) and switch the whole response toward shutdown.<Cite n={2} /> Resomelagon is a
    deliberate attempt to bottle that: a drug that nudges the immune system to resolve cleanly
    rather than one that clamps it down and leaves it there. For the broader biology this sits
    inside, see the <a href="/inflammation">fourth axis — melanocortin control of inflammation</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The rheumatoid arthritis story</h2>
  <p>
    Resomelagon’s lead indication is <strong>rheumatoid arthritis</strong>, and its clinical arc
    there is an honest lesson in how a real frontier behaves — not a straight line up. Three
    studies tell it:
  </p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Study</th>
          <th>Design</th>
          <th>Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>BEGIN</td>
          <td>Early Phase 2, resomelagon alone in newly diagnosed RA</td>
          <td>Missed in the full group; signal in patients with high systemic inflammation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>EXPAND</td>
          <td>Phase 2a, resomelagon + methotrexate, DMARD-naïve RA</td>
          <td>82% ACR20 (n=28) vs 52% on placebo + MTX (n=27)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ADVANCE</td>
          <td>Phase 2b, resomelagon + methotrexate (2026)</td>
          <td>Primary DAS28-CRP endpoint not met; 40 mg ACR20 76.4% vs 60.8% placebo</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The combination study, <strong>EXPAND</strong>, was the encouraging one: added to methotrexate
    in patients new to disease-modifying drugs, resomelagon pushed the ACR20 response — the
    standard "20% better" bar in RA trials — to 82%, against 52% on methotrexate alone.<Cite n={4} />
    The larger confirmatory Phase 2b, <strong>ADVANCE</strong>, is where it got hard. The trial
    <strong>missed its primary endpoint</strong> on DAS28-CRP, undercut by an unusually strong
    placebo response that narrowed the gap.<Cite n={5} /> That is the headline, and it should be
    read as the setback it is.
  </p>
  <p>
    What keeps the program alive is everything underneath that headline. At the 40 mg dose the
    ACR20 response was 76.4% versus 60.8% on placebo, the effect reached significance in the
    pre-specified ACR/EULAR class II–III subgroup that made up most of the trial, inflammatory
    markers like CRP fell meaningfully, and the safety profile stayed clean — notably with no
    evidence of the immunosuppression that dogs conventional RA drugs.<Cite n={5} /> Analysts have
    read the readout as genuinely mixed rather than fatal: enough to select a dose, and enough to
    justify an end-of-Phase-2 conversation with the FDA about a <strong>Phase 3</strong>
    design.<Cite n={6} /> Whether that confirms or collapses is exactly the open question — the
    kind this field is full of.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond arthritis</h2>
  <p>
    Because the mechanism is about resolving inflammation in general, resomelagon is not a
    one-disease drug on paper. SynAct has taken it into <strong>idiopathic membranous
    nephropathy</strong> — an autoimmune kidney disease that drives nephrotic-range protein loss —
    in a Phase 2a study, and into <strong>host-directed antiviral</strong> work (the RESOVIR
    program), testing whether calming the immune overreaction to a respiratory infection can speed
    recovery.<Cite n={1} /> These are earlier and more speculative than the arthritis program, but
    they show the shape of the bet: one pro-resolution switch, aimed wherever an inflammatory
    response needs help finishing rather than suppressing.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it sits</h2>
  <p>
    Resomelagon is one of a small cluster of drugs trying to turn the melanocortin
    anti-inflammatory axis into medicine. It runs alongside Palatin’s <a href="/pl-8177">PL-8177</a>{' '}
    (an oral MC1R agonist aimed locally at the colon in ulcerative colitis) and{' '}
    <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a> (an MC1R agonist studied for the fibrosis of systemic
    sclerosis) — each a different way of asking the same system to quiet a different organ. What
    sets resomelagon apart is the explicit bet on biased agonism as the way to keep the benefit and
    drop the baggage. Track all of them, dated and sourced, on the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline page</a>.
  </p>

